[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced .NET: Delegates, Events, Lambdas, and Expression Trees"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial explores the foundational pillars of functional programming and dynamic code execution in .NET. Understanding how delegates, events, lambdas, and expression trees work together is essential for writing efficient, extensible, and high-performance C# applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Delegates"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A delegate is a type that safely encapsulates a method, similar to a function pointer in C++. It defines a signature, and any method assigned to the delegate must match that signature."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public delegate int MathOperation(int a, int b);

public class Calculator
{
    public static int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;
    public static int Multiply(int a, int b) => a * b;
}

// Usage:
MathOperation op = Calculator.Add;
int result = op(3, 4); // Returns 7"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Events: The Observer Pattern"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"An event is a wrapper around a delegate that provides security and encapsulation. It ensures that only the containing class can trigger the event, while external subscribers can only add or remove handlers."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public class ProcessEventArgs : EventArgs
{
    public string Message { get; set; }
}

public class ProcessWorker
{
    public event EventHandler<ProcessEventArgs> ProcessCompleted;

    public void StartWork()
    {
        // Do work...
        OnProcessCompleted(new ProcessEventArgs { Message = "Success" });
    }

    protected virtual void OnProcessCompleted(ProcessEventArgs e)
    {
        ProcessCompleted?.Invoke(this, e);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Lambda Expressions"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lambda expressions provide an inline syntax for writing anonymous methods. They significantly reduce the boilerplate code required when working with delegates or LINQ queries."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"Func<int, int, int> add = (a, b) => a + b;
int sum = add(5, 10); // Returns 15

List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
var evens = numbers.Where(n => n % 2 == 0).ToList();"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Expression Trees"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"While lambda expressions compile directly to executable IL code, expression trees represent lambda expressions as data structures (in-memory data). This allows code to be inspected, modified, and translated at runtime, which is heavily used by ORMs like Entity Framework to translate C# queries into SQL."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"using System.Linq.Expressions;

Expression<Func<int, int, int>> expr = (a, b) => a + b;
BinaryExpression body = (BinaryExpression)expr.Body;

ParameterExpression left = (ParameterExpression)body.Left;
ParameterExpression right = (ParameterExpression)body.Right;

Console.WriteLine(body.NodeType); // Output: Add
Console.WriteLine(left.Name);      // Output: a

// Compile and execute the expression tree
Func<int, int, int> compiled = expr.Compile();
int result = compiled(10, 20); // Returns 30"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Key Differences"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Delegates define the signature for reference-based method calls."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Events build on delegates to provide safe publisher-subscriber mechanics."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lambda expressions offer concise syntax for inline anonymous functions."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Expression trees turn code into inspectable data structures for advanced manipulation and translation."}]
